{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT CAUSE — confirmed, reproduced deterministically on the first attempt\n\nNot a capture race, not handler teardown, and NOT the `confirm_store is None` path this\nticket originally suspected. It is a **leaked logger LEVEL on the sibling `rebar_reconciler`\nroot**, which the suite's existing caplog containment guard does not cover.\n\n### Mechanism\n\nThe two log lines in the failure differ only in LEVEL, and both fire on the same code path:\n\n* per-item — `logger.warning(...)` in `_inbound_unlink_one`\n  (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py:578`)\n* pass summary — `logger.info(...)` at the tail of `_inbound_update_apply_links`\n  (same file, ~line 790)\n\nBoth use `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)` = `rebar_reconciler.apply_inbound_records`.\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:606` calls\n`install_stderr_handler(\"rebar_reconciler\")`, and `src/rebar/_logging.py:88` does\n`logger.setLevel(level)` — pinning the shared **`rebar_reconciler`** logger to WARNING.\nLoggers are process-global singletons and nothing restores it, so the first test in an xdist\nworker that drives the reconciler entrypoint in-process poisons every later test in that\nworker.\n\n`caplog.at_level(logging.INFO)` **cannot** rescue this: with no `logger=` argument it raises\nthe ROOT logger's level, not the intermediate `rebar_reconciler` logger's. The INFO record is\ntherefore dropped by `isEnabledFor` at the originating logger, before any handler is\nconsulted, while the WARNING record still propagates to caplog's root handler. That is\nexactly the observed asymmetry — summary missing, per-item line present — and it explains why\n`confirm_store` was never the culprit (if it had been `None`, the per-item WARNING would have\nbeen missing too, since that line is emitted only when the store is non-None).\n\n### Why the existing guard missed it\n\nThe repo already documents this exact vector and already contains it: `tests/_log_integrity.py`\n(`current_level` / `restore_level`) plus the autouse `_rebar_log_propagation_guard` in\n`tests/conftest.py`. But both are hard-coded to a single root,\n`REBAR_LOGGER_NAME = \"rebar\"` — while `src/rebar/_logging.py`'s own module docstring names\n**two** shared roots (\"the sibling `rebar_reconciler` root for the reconciler subprocess,\nwhich is imported top-level as `rebar_reconciler.*`\"). Everything under `rebar_reconciler.*`\nwas simply outside the containment.\n\n### Named polluter + reproduction (AC1, RED)\n\n```\npytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_main_entry.py \\\n       tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_inbound_removal_confirmation_a4bd.py \\\n       -p no:randomly\n=> 1 failed, 15 passed\nAssertionError: ['_apply_inbound_update: declining the inbound removal of blocks link ...']\n```\n\nDeterministic, first try — the identical assertion text CI reported. `test_main_entry.py` is\none instance, not the unique one: any test that reaches the reconciler `main()` in-process\ndoes it, which is why worksteal made shard/run selection look random.\n\n### AC4 — is the `declined` count a production defect?\n\n**No, correct by design.** In `_inbound_update_apply_links` the counter increments only under\n`elif confirm_store is not None and not confirm_store.is_confirmed(...)`. When the store is\n`None` the confirmation guard in `_inbound_unlink_one` is bypassed entirely, so the removal is\n**applied**, not declined — there is nothing to report and `declined == 0` is truthful, not\nan under-report. The summary is suppressed because no decline happened. No production\nlogging behaviour is changed by this fix."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786611687596498001,"uuid":"a53f9d6a-faa7-4fc5-8bea-837880c4384e"}